\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{An odd quadratic integral is a Pfaffian}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{\Omega} be the image in #{\Lambda^2} of an alternating
bilinear form on a #{2m}-dimensional space, as in the [Pfaffian of signed pairings](fdip-008I).
The top-degree component of #{\ue^{-\Omega}} is
#{(-1)^m\Omega^m/m!}. For the volume #{\omega} used to define the
scalar Pfaffian, the coefficient of #{\omega} in #{\ue^{-\Omega}} is
#{(-1)^m\operatorname{Pf}_\omega}. The identity does not use an
inverse. If the form is nondegenerate, the Pfaffian element is
nonzero, so the scalar is nonzero for every nonzero volume. The
zero-dimensional value is #{1}. \ref{fdip-00ME} recovers
##{I_\xi\left(\ue^{-\frac12\xi^TA\xi}\right)=\operatorname{Pf}(A)}
for the reversed chart, including both signs.}

\proof{
\p{Put #{\Omega=\sum_{i<j}A_{ij}\xi_i\xi_j
=\xi^TA\xi/2}. In #{\Omega^m}, a nonzero top word uses
each generator exactly once. The even two-letter factors commute;
every matching appears in #{m!} orders. Reordering its word to
#{\xi_1\cdots\xi_{2m}} gives its matching sign. Therefore
##{\frac{\Omega^m}{m!}
 =\operatorname{Pf}(A)\xi_1\cdots\xi_{2m}.}
The top term of #{\ue^{-\Omega}} is #{(-1)^m\Omega^m/m!}.
Reversing #{2m} generators has sign
#{(-1)^{m(2m-1)}=(-1)^m}; the two signs cancel. This proves
the integral identity, without an inverse.}

\p{For the nonvanishing assertion, regard #{A} as a nondegenerate
alternating form on the vector space dual to the generators.
Choose a nonzero #{u} and then #{v} with #{A(u,v)=1}.
The plane they span is nondegenerate, and its orthogonal complement
for #{A} is a direct complement on which #{A} remains
nondegenerate. Induction gives a basis of such pairs. In the dual
basis #{\alpha_1,\beta_1,\ldots,\alpha_m,\beta_m},
##{\Omega=\sum_{r=1}^m\alpha_r\beta_r,\qquad
 \frac{\Omega^m}{m!}=\alpha_1\beta_1\cdots\alpha_m\beta_m\ne0.}
An invertible change of generators multiplies a top word by a
nonzero determinant. The displayed top coefficient is therefore
nonzero in the original basis as well, proving
#{\operatorname{Pf}(A)\ne0}. The zero-dimensional case is #{1}.}
}

\p{In \citet{v2, Proposition 9.9, p. 131}{etingof2024mathematical},
the increasing top order accompanies a positive quadratic
exponent. Replacing #{A} by #{-A} contributes #{(-1)^m} to
the Pfaffian, and reversing the top order contributes the same
sign. Thus the convention conversion agrees with the direct proof.}

\transclude{fdip-00ME}
